def build(course, m):
    return course(94, 'Marine Conservation, Justice and Participation',
        'Marine Conservation,\nJustice and\nParticipation',
        'Appraise the fairness of a fictional marine conservation decision and design meaningful participation and redress.', [
        m('Assess recognition, voice and distribution',
            'Use three equity questions to examine a proposed conservation measure.',
            """
Equitable conservation asks who and what is recognised, how decisions are made and who gains or bears costs. A plan may recognise boat owners while omitting a traditional shore harvest or a seasonal walking route. Record where groups use the area, what they depend on and how they describe it. Seek appropriate permission before sharing sensitive information about a real location. [S83]

Procedural equity concerns meaningful access to information, influence, accountable decision roles and ways to question a decision. Distribution concerns where restrictions, fees, benefits or opportunities land. A consultation site may be physically inaccessible while a benefit programme unintentionally favours people with a formal membership. These are distinct problems with different practical remedies. [S83] [R92]

The global biodiversity target concerns effective conservation and equitable governance, with rights and sustainable use conditions. Reading a global coverage goal into a fictional map does not tell learners which activities are legally allowed at a site. Examine the proposed governance and ecological evidence, and refer unresolved tenure, conservation and access decisions to the competent bodies. [S83]

Create an equity record for a zone proposal. Include a use or group that is unrecognised, a specific influence barrier and an example of an uneven cost. Name the authorised role and the question each gap raises. Discuss how the proposal could be changed before it is finalised. If a decision is closed, represent that honestly and identify the available review route. [S81] Check whether transition support is available to workers with limited evidence of former earnings. A payment route needs both appropriate eligibility and practical access.
            """,
            'A conservation plan records boats but omits shore harvesters. Notices are online only. A transition benefit is reserved for formal association members.',
            'Classify one issue under recognition, procedure and distribution, then propose a response for each.',
            'Record shore use, improve access to decision participation and review benefit criteria against actual dependence.',
            """
Recognise forms of use.|Which example shows a recognition gap?|The resource map leaves out seasonal shore harvesters.|A review date appears on the notice.|A map identifies a known access path.|The minutes record divergent views.|Recognition concerns whose uses and identities are visible.
Make decisions accessible.|Which scenario points first to a procedural barrier?|Online-only notices fail to reach workers who can use a staffed venue.|One group receives a larger transition grant.|Storm exposure differs by location.|The designation extends across two maps.|Procedural equity concerns information and influence.
Trace distribution.|Which question addresses distribution of transition costs?|Who pays or loses earnings when the restriction begins?|Was the meeting invitation readable?|Who can request the minutes?|Was the map properly dated?|Distribution asks who receives benefits and bears burdens.
Connect coverage to outcomes.|What else must a zone reviewer consider beyond its area?|Conservation performance, governance and rights.|Only the area on the promotional map.|The campaign logo.|The number of sponsors alone.|Area does not describe effectiveness or governance.
Check decision status.|How should a closed legal decision be described in a meeting?|Explain the status and any genuine review or implementation choices.|Promise a vote can reopen everything.|Say that attendance grants approval.|Offer choices the team cannot change.|Participants should understand what they can influence.
            """),
        m('Support participatory coastal planning',
            'Design a participation process with access, representation and a feedback record.',
            """
Begin with what decision is open, whose authority applies and which groups have rights, needs or knowledge relevant to it. A worker who gathers shoreline knowledge should ask what can be shared, with whom and for what purpose. Some information may expose a sensitive harvesting site or a person in conflict. Agree how to record the contribution and any limits. [S83]

Provide understandable materials in time for people to respond. Use accessible meeting locations, alternative channels and arrangements for people who cannot attend at the advertised hour. In the Lagos research, inclusion and warning access were recurring concerns among selected coastal participants. Use these concerns to ask what local engagement might miss and avoid assuming one resident speaks for everyone. [R92]

Build a response log that links each substantive concern to a proposed action, decision or reason it cannot be addressed. Mark where there is disagreement and where further input is necessary. Participation does not require complete agreement. It requires accurate representation, traceable reasons and a way for affected people to see how their participation was handled. [S81]

In the exercise, compare a draft map with a revision after a meeting. A changed loading time may address one group while adding a burden for another. Record the reason for revision and retest it with the affected groups. Maintain a route for late, confidential or dissenting responses, with an appropriately authorised decision role and a clear review date. [S83] Share the updated map in a format people can understand and check whether they can locate the places where their work could change.
            """,
            'A meeting maps fishing routes while many shore workers are trading. The promoter publishes the raw map with confidential details and records no response to objections.',
            'Design accessible participation and a versioned feedback record. Specify what spatial information can be shared.',
            'State scope and information permissions. Offer another participation route and show how each concern led to a decision or follow-up.',
            """
Describe what can change.|Which fact should a participation notice explain first?|The decision open to input and who will make it.|The number of tea breaks.|A guarantee that every view will be accepted.|The font planned for the final map.|Participants need a clear scope for influence.
Respect information limits.|How should sensitive knowledge enter the planning exercise?|Agree purpose and sharing limits before using an appropriately generalised account.|Publish exact details by default.|Record confidential detail in publicity.|Share personal identifiers with every participant.|Information use should respect the contribution and avoid unnecessary exposure.
Address attendance barriers.|Which change helps traders unable to attend during sales?|Offer another practical time or accessible route for comments.|Assume absence means agreement.|Move the meeting to the busiest hour.|Exclude written or oral alternatives.|Different routes improve opportunities to participate.
Show reasons and responses.|Which output makes participation traceable?|A concern-to-response log with reason and decision owner.|A photograph of the meeting only.|A count that merges support and objections.|Minutes hiding recorded disagreement.|Feedback should show how input affected the decision.
Retest effects of revisions.|What should the team do after changing the loading window?|Check consequences for traders, crew and other affected groups.|Treat the first revision as sufficient proof.|Erase the earlier draft and its rationale.|Ask only the project sponsor.|Revisions can transfer constraints and need review.
            """),
        m('Design grievance and remedy follow-up',
            'Build a safe concern route and verify whether the agreed response reached people.',
            """
A grievance route starts with a way to raise concerns safely and receive an understandable response. Identify who can submit, what channels are available and who can review a concern independently of the original decision. Give a clear acknowledgement and expected next step, including a private option when public discussion would expose someone. [S83]

For a fictional conservation restriction, record whether the concern is about information, barriers to access, an eligibility decision or implementation. A correction to an inaccurate map may be possible within the project. A dispute about legal authority or tenure should go through the relevant formal process. Show how the initial route connects to competent review without promising a particular result. [S81]

A remedy needs an owner, timeframe and evidence of delivery. If a project agrees to restore working access at a jetty, check whether the new route is safely usable at the relevant time. If a transition grant is agreed, track the responsible service and actual receipt under its rules. An agreement on paper shows a commitment at a stage of the process. [S84]

Report aggregated information about concerns received, time to response and unresolved issues. A low number of complaints may reflect successful implementation, poor access to the route or fear of adverse consequences. Keep a review window and use multiple safe ways to understand the experience of affected people. Monitor both agreed remedy and the practical effect on the livelihood or access question. [S83] Include a route to correct the record if someone says the summary misrepresents their concern. A count alone cannot show whether a response was fair.
            """,
            'The same person who approved a closure dismisses access complaints. The dashboard counts a proposed replacement path as a resolved remedy.',
            'Specify an accessible complaint route, responsible review, escalation and two outcome checks.',
            'Assign an independent review route and check whether the replacement path is usable when workers need it.',
            """
Make the route safe and usable.|What supports a worker with a sensitive access complaint?|An accessible private channel with acknowledgement and clear next steps.|A public form that broadcasts the account.|A requirement to confront the decision maker alone.|A social media post with names.|A practical route supports confidential participation.
Separate types of complaint.|Who should decide a real unresolved legal tenure question?|The competent authorised forum.|The learner preparing the map.|An informal promoter without authority.|A meeting attendee selected at random.|Different grievance subjects require different authority.
Check independence.|Which feature improves review of a contested original decision?|A defined reviewer separate from the decision under challenge where appropriate.|The same officer automatically closes every case.|The sponsor is the only appeal route.|A system assigns no review responsibility.|Independence can support an impartial response.
Verify remedy delivery.|Which result best demonstrates restored access?|Workers can use the verified replacement path during the needed hours.|A contractor promised to draw it.|A report uses the word resolved.|The route was discussed at a meeting.|A delivered remedy is observable in the agreed conditions.
Interpret complaint volume carefully.|Why might few grievances coexist with serious barriers?|People may lack a safe or accessible complaint route.|Every person must agree with the decision.|An empty register certifies full fairness.|Complaint counts show all livelihood outcomes.|The route itself influences recorded complaints.
            """),
    ])
